m2_alpha_llm/test: the scripted chat test

chat_test.md (one directory up) is the conversation to hold with the
model to exercise every tool, the plans, the teams and the contact set,
with what to expect of each line. chat_driver.py holds that conversation
for you against a live fleet and grades it; chat_tests.txt is the same
script in the form the driver reads.

  ./chat_driver.py                 headless, warp 10, every test
  ./chat_driver.py --gui           pMarineViewer up, warp 5, so you watch
  ./chat_driver.py --gui --step    and it waits for the NEXT_TEST button
                                   (the viewer's fourth button, also in the
                                   Action menu) before each test
  ./chat_driver.py --only 1.1,3.1  a subset, --amt 2 for two boats,
                                   --keep to leave the fleet up afterwards

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY must be exported: pLLMAgent reads it, the driver never
sees it. Each run copies the mission into test/runs/<stamp>/ (ignored by
git), launches abe, ben, cal on ports 9101.. with the shoreside on 9100
(so a mission of your own on 9000 can stay up), and writes results.md
and results.json there beside the run's LOG_ and XLOG_ directories.

How it works. The driver types each test's line into the chat by
posting LLM_CHAT_IN with uPokeDB; the chat pane shows such a line as
"uPokeDB> ...". It then reads the shoreside alog as it grows: a
proposal is an LLM_CHAT_OUT line from pLLMAgent:ask and is answered y
or n (the test's answer); a plan's Ask is a BT_CHAT line with the ask
mark and is answered with plan_answer; a plain reply ends the turn.
Then it waits for the test's wait lines (MISSION_ABE = goto_complete,
BT_STATE_RED = success ...) as NEW posts, grades the checks listed in
chat_tests.txt from the log lines since the test's mark (tool calls,
targets, the proposal's wording, reply length, collisions and near
misses, closest ranges, plan states, latency, truncation), and posts
the verdict as a text box at the top left of the map. Nothing older
than the test's start counts, so a value left by an earlier test never
satisfies a later wait.

Expectations must allow for the model's variety: "tool goto|route", a
sentence cap, a question mark, never an exact sentence. A failed test
names the check that failed and what was seen; the run's XLOG holds the
full story (LLM_TOOL_CALL, LLM_CHAT_OUT, LLM_USAGE, COLLISION ...).
